# macOS notarization credentials.
#
# Copy this file to `electron-builder.env` and fill in ONE of the three blocks
# below. `electron-builder.env` is git-ignored — never commit real credentials.
# The electron-builder CLI loads it automatically from the project root, and
# `npm run notarize:dmg` reads the same file.
#
# Values already present in the real environment win over this file.
#
# The code signing certificate itself is NOT configured here. Import the
# "Developer ID Application" certificate into the login keychain and
# electron-builder finds it on its own. See docs/MACOS-SIGNING.md.

# --- Option 1 (recommended for local builds): keychain profile --------------
# Nothing secret is stored on disk. Create the profile once with:
#
#   xcrun notarytool store-credentials "realm-studio-plus" \
#     --apple-id "you@example.com" \
#     --team-id "YOURTEAMID" \
#     --password "app-specific-password"
#
# After that the app-specific password lives in the login keychain only, and
# this file just names the profile.
APPLE_KEYCHAIN_PROFILE=realm-studio-plus

# --- Option 2: App Store Connect API key ------------------------------------
# APPLE_API_KEY points at the .p8 file. Keep that file outside the repository.
# Scoped to App Store Connect and revocable on its own, unlike an Apple ID.
# APPLE_API_KEY=/Users/you/.private_keys/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
# APPLE_API_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX
# APPLE_API_ISSUER=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# --- Option 3: Apple ID + app-specific password -----------------------------
# Simplest, but the weakest isolation: an app-specific password grants access
# to the whole Apple ID, including iCloud Mail, Contacts and Calendar. It is
# also passed to `notarytool` as a command line argument, so it is visible in
# the process list while the build runs. Prefer option 1 or 2.
# APPLE_ID=you@example.com
# APPLE_APP_SPECIFIC_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=YOURTEAMID
